==German==

===Etymology===
A dialectal pronunciation of {{m|de|Raum}} from {{inh|de|gmh|roum}}, from {{inh|de|goh|roum}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*raumaz}}, ousting the regular form due to identicalness with {{m|de|Raum|t=room}} (from {{noncog|gmh|rūm}}). Dialects that have developed Middle High German ''-ou-'' into ''-ā-'' are found in south-eastern and central-western Germany. Cognate with {{cog|nl|room}} and {{cog|en|ream}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ʁaːm/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-Rahm.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}
* {{rhymes|aːm|lang=de}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|m||gen2=Rahmes|-}}

# [[cream]] (milkfat)

====Usage notes====
In German standard German, ''Rahm'' is used mainly in compounds (such as [[Rahmsoße]], [[Sauerrahm]] etc.); otherwise {{m|de|Sahne}} is much more common. This is not necessarily true of colloquial varieties nor of standard German in Austria and Switzerland.

